{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012J36SxfE7CifM5Uhnn9xWe","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on my search of the context filesystem, I found the answer to your question about the KRaft timeout-metrics design discussion:\\\\n\\\\n## Answer\\\\n\\\\n**The design decision is: Metrics should ALWAYS be reported and only increment when the timer goes from unexpired to expired during poll().**\\\\n\\\\n### Key Discussion Points\\\\n\\\\nFrom **`/store/kips/KIP-1322.md`** (lines 20-22):\\\\n\\\\n- **Key discussion point**: Whether metrics should only be reported when the underlying timer exists in a particular EpochState, or should always report values\\\\n- **Resolution**: Discussion clarified that metrics should always be reported and only increment when the timer goes from unexpired to expired during poll()\\\\n\\\\n### Additional Context\\\\n\\\\nThe KIP-1322 proposes adding metrics to KRaft to track:\\\\n- Fetch timeout expiration counts  \\\\n- Election timeout expiration counts\\\\n\\\\nThese metrics help operators monitor the health of KRaft consensus groups by understanding timeout behavior.\\\\n\\\\nThe design discussion occurred on the dev mailing list, with contributions discussing implementation details including metric naming, types, and **when metrics should be reported based on node state**. The final decision favored the approach of **always reporting** metrics regardless of state, rather than only reporting them when specific state conditions exist.\\\\n\\\\n**Sources cited:**\\\\n- `/store/kips/KIP-1322.md`\\\\n- `/store/issues/KAFKA-20513.md`\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"store\\\",\\\"kips/KIP-1322.md\\\",\\\"issues/KAFKA-20513.md\\\",\\\"INDEX-WINDOW-100.md\\\",\\\"WINDOW-106-2026-04-29-30-summary.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":17.18,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":2,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":64386,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":16214,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.0451366,\\\"input_tokens\\\":50,\\\"output_tokens\\\":1244}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7df63789-81c2-45cc-a649-a517f8234063","uuid":"ed4055ef-6dec-48e4-bf86-20e13024016c","timestamp":"2026-09-27T19:23:12.228Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"Based on my search of the context filesystem, I found the answer to your question about the KRaft timeout-metrics design discussion:\\\\n\\\\n## Answer\\\\n\\\\n**The design decision is: Metrics should ALWAYS be reported and only increment when the timer goes from unexpired to expired during poll().**\\\\n\\\\n### Key Discussion Points\\\\n\\\\nFrom **`/store/kips/KIP-1322.md`** (lines 20-22):\\\\n\\\\n- **Key discussion point**: Whether metrics should only be reported when the underlying timer exists in a particular EpochState, or should always report values\\\\n- **Resolution**: Discussion clarified that metrics should always be reported and only increment when the timer goes from unexpired to expired during poll()\\\\n\\\\n### Additional Context\\\\n\\\\nThe KIP-1322 proposes adding metrics to KRaft to track:\\\\n- Fetch timeout expiration counts  \\\\n- Election timeout expiration counts\\\\n\\\\nThese metrics help operators monitor the health of KRaft consensus groups by understanding timeout behavior.\\\\n\\\\nThe design discussion occurred on the dev mailing list, with contributions discussing implementation details including metric naming, types, and **when metrics should be reported based on node state**. The final decision favored the approach of **always reporting** metrics regardless of state, rather than only reporting them when specific state conditions exist.\\\\n\\\\n**Sources cited:**\\\\n- `/store/kips/KIP-1322.md`\\\\n- `/store/issues/KAFKA-20513.md`\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"store\\\",\\\"kips/KIP-1322.md\\\",\\\"issues/KAFKA-20513.md\\\",\\\"INDEX-WINDOW-100.md\\\",\\\"WINDOW-106-2026-04-29-30-summary.md\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":17.18,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":2,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":64386,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":16214,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.0451366,\\\"input_tokens\\\":50,\\\"output_tokens\\\":1244}}\"}","_meta":{"fastmcp":{"wrap_result":true}},"structuredContent":{"result":"{\"answer\":\"Based on my search of the context filesystem, I found the answer to your question about the KRaft timeout-metrics design discussion:\\n\\n## Answer\\n\\n**The design decision is: Metrics should ALWAYS be reported and only increment when the timer goes from unexpired to expired during poll().**\\n\\n### Key Discussion Points\\n\\nFrom **`/store/kips/KIP-1322.md`** (lines 20-22):\\n\\n- **Key discussion point**: Whether metrics should only be reported when the underlying timer exists in a particular EpochState, or should always report values\\n- **Resolution**: Discussion clarified that metrics should always be reported and only increment when the timer goes from unexpired to expired during poll()\\n\\n### Additional Context\\n\\nThe KIP-1322 proposes adding metrics to KRaft to track:\\n- Fetch timeout expiration counts  \\n- Election timeout expiration counts\\n\\nThese metrics help operators monitor the health of KRaft consensus groups by understanding timeout behavior.\\n\\nThe design discussion occurred on the dev mailing list, with contributions discussing implementation details including metric naming, types, and **when metrics should be reported based on node state**. The final decision favored the approach of **always reporting** metrics regardless of state, rather than only reporting them when specific state conditions exist.\\n\\n**Sources cited:**\\n- `/store/kips/KIP-1322.md`\\n- `/store/issues/KAFKA-20513.md`\",\"error\":null,\"files\":[\"store\",\"kips/KIP-1322.md\",\"issues/KAFKA-20513.md\",\"INDEX-WINDOW-100.md\",\"WINDOW-106-2026-04-29-30-summary.md\"],\"seconds\":17.18,\"truncated\":false,\"turns\":2,\"usage\":{\"cache_read_tokens\":64386,\"cache_write_tokens\":16214,\"cost_